1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
Okay, moving on to dementia and degenerative disorders.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:06,920
The key focus here is going to be on the word degenerative,

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,060
and what you need to understand is that in these particular disorders,

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:13,760
what's going to happen is you're going to lose or degenerate neurons.

5
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:17,000
Now, let's go back to just a little bit of basics.

6
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:20,540
So imagine for a moment that this is a coronal section through the brain.

7
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:25,040
Of course, you have gray matter, and then underneath that gray matter, there's white matter,

8
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:29,520
and then, of course, for example, here are ventricles, so there are two ventricles here,

9
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:33,600
so these would be the lateral ventricles, and then adjacent to the ventricles,

10
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:35,480
you often have additional gray matter.

11
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:40,680
Now, this additional gray matter represents the deeper neurons present within the brain,

12
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:46,320
and what you need to know about the degenerative disorders is that you're going to obviously degenerate neurons,

13
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:49,240
and so the degeneration can occur up here in the cortex,

14
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:55,000
and when you degenerate the neurons of the cortex, that's going to result in dementia.

15
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:59,460
In addition, you can degenerate the neurons in the deeper structures of the brain,

16
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:03,980
and in particular, the key structure that we're going to focus on is something called the basal ganglia.

17
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:10,200
The basal ganglia are neurons present within the deeper brain that are important for movement,

18
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:15,760
and therefore, if you degenerate the neurons of the basal ganglia, that's going to result in movement problems.

19
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:21,340
And so this is the paradigm that you need to have in your mind concerning the degenerative disorders.

20
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:23,920
Now, of course, I'm going to give you much more detail as we move forward,

21
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:27,140
but this is just to lay down a basic principle in your mind.

22
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,040
Now, with that, let's begin to text.

23
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:33,600
So I say that degenerative disorders are characterized by the loss of neurons,

24
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:36,120
and, of course, that's going to be within the gray matter,

25
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:38,480
and you can lose neurons within the cortex,

26
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:41,420
and when you lose neurons within the gray matter of the cortex,

27
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:43,180
that's going to result in dementia,

28
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:47,060
or you can lose neurons within the brainstem and the basal ganglia,

29
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:49,860
but really the key idea here is the basal ganglia,

30
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:54,820
and when you lose these neurons, that's going to result in movement disorders.

31
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:58,100
The first of these disorders is Alzheimer's disease.

32
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:02,500
This is a degenerative disease which involves the cortex,

33
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:06,060
in particular the neurons present in the gray matter of the cortex,

34
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:11,140
and, of course, when you involve the cortex, you're going to create dementia, as I've just explained.

35
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:15,300
And it so happens that Alzheimer's disease is the most common cause of dementia.

36
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:19,160
Now, to understand Alzheimer's disease, you do need a little bit of a background,

37
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,660
so let's take a step back.

38
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:24,940
This is a neuron, and I'm drawing for you the cell membrane of the neuron,

39
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,340
and present on this cell membrane of the neuron is a receptor,

40
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:33,180
which is derived from a protein called amyloid precursor protein.

41
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:35,720
Now, we don't know what this receptor does,

42
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:39,360
but what we do know is that when this receptor is being broken down,

43
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:41,980
remember that it's a protein that's going to be used,

44
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:44,840
and over time it's going to be broken down and then recycled.

45
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:49,780
So when this protein is broken down, it normally is broken down by an alpha secretase enzyme,

46
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:55,060
which results in an alpha byproduct, which can then be turned over without any problem.

47
00:02:55,640 --> 00:03:00,100
However, sometimes it can be broken down by a beta secretase,

48
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:02,020
which results in a beta product,

49
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:05,460
and the problem with that beta product is that it cannot be turned over,

50
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:08,060
and instead it deposits as an amyloid,

51
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:11,440
and this is called A-beta amyloid.

52
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:15,320
So A-beta amyloid, when it's produced, cannot be broken down,

53
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:17,880
and then it begins to deposit within the brain,

54
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:23,200
and it's the deposition of A-beta amyloid that then results in Alzheimer's disease.

55
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:27,240
Again, very simplistically, amyloid precursor protein gets cleaved.

56
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:29,680
That results in A-beta amyloid.

57
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:34,400
A-beta amyloid deposits within the brain, resulting in Alzheimer's disease.

58
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:38,120
The clinical features of Alzheimer's disease are all based on dementia.

59
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:45,800
Now remember, dementia is memory loss plus cognitive dysfunction without any loss of consciousness.

60
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:48,640
So this is sort of the definition of dementia.

61
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:52,200
And so these patients get a slow-onset memory loss.

62
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:53,960
It's important to note that it's slow-onset.

63
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:55,440
It takes years to develop.

64
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:59,280
And it begins with recent memory, and then it progresses to distant memory.

65
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:02,120
Over time, they get progressive disorientation.

66
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:08,500
They begin to develop cognitive dysfunction and begin to lose motor skills and language skills.

67
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:12,560
In addition, they can develop behavior or personality changes.

68
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,780
Eventually, the patients become mute and bedridden.

69
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:20,600
And what's very important to note about Alzheimer's disease, especially early in the disease,

70
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:24,620
is you do not get focal neurologic deficits.

71
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:33,640
So for example, you wouldn't see features of Parkinson's disease, such as a pill-rolling tremor, or akinesia, or an expressionless face.

72
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:41,520
In addition, you wouldn't see features of Huntington's disease, like chorea, which are these random purposeless movements of proximal muscle.

73
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:46,180
So focal neurologic deficits will not be seen early in this disease.

74
00:04:46,180 --> 00:04:51,140
When you consider the epidemiology of Alzheimer's disease, most cases are sporadic.

75
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:57,200
I'd like to go take a step back and just remind you that if this is Alzheimer's disease, you have the sporadic form,

76
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:01,240
and then you also have something called the early form.

77
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:06,820
Now, the key feature in the sporadic form is that the major risk factor is age.

78
00:05:07,220 --> 00:05:11,740
So with time, there's an increased risk of developing the sporadic form.

79
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:15,680
And, of course, this sporadic form is predominantly a disease of the elderly.

80
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:27,300
Another important association here is that the risk is increased with the epsilon-4 allele of apolipoprotein E.

81
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:29,240
And that's particularly high yield.

82
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:31,200
Board examiners really like to go after that.

83
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:38,580
This epsilon-4 allele increases the conversion of amyloid precursor protein to the beta form.

84
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:46,600
And that then, of course, results in extra deposition of that beta form, resulting in A-beta amyloid, which then, of course, results in Alzheimer's disease.

85
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:55,520
And another thing that examiners like you to know is that there is a decreased risk with the epsilon-2 allele of apolipoprotein E.

86
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:57,960
And so that's a particularly high yield association.

87
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:03,440
Now, if you're having trouble remembering that, it's kind of easy because 4 is higher than 2.

88
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:09,380
And so 4 has the increased risk, and the epsilon-2 has a decreased risk, if that helps you.

89
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:13,080
The second important form is the early onset form.

90
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:18,960
And in the early onset form, you don't necessarily see this in the elderly, but you see it in patients who are younger.

91
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:20,960
And that's what we mean by early onset.

92
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:31,520
Now, the key important associations here are, number one, there is a familial form in which patients have a genetic mutation that's transferred through the family.

93
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:37,080
And the key genetic mutation that results in early onset Alzheimer's disease is called presenilin.

94
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:41,640
Now, there are two genes that can be involved, presenilin-1 and presenilin-2.

95
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,140
And the key mutation is actually present within presenilin-1.

96
00:06:46,140 --> 00:06:53,020
So mutations in presenilin-1 can result in familial cases of Alzheimer's disease, which would give you early onset of disease.

97
00:06:53,620 --> 00:06:58,360
The second association here is that there's an increased risk of the early form in patients with Down syndrome.

98
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:04,800
In fact, by the age of 40, a large number of patients with Down syndrome will have developed Alzheimer's disease.

99
00:07:05,220 --> 00:07:08,320
Now, this is particularly high yield, and examiners love to go after it.

100
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:13,680
One of the essential principles here is that, of course, in Down syndrome, you have three copies of chromosome 21.

101
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:24,680
And remember, and very high yield, that the amyloid precursor protein that eventually results in the formation of the A-beta amyloid is actually present on chromosome 21.

102
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:32,500
And so, because the patients with Down syndrome have three copies of chromosome 21, they have an extra copy of the amyloid precursor protein,

103
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:36,440
and hence there is an increased production of the A-beta amyloid.

104
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:39,840
And so that, again, is a particularly high yield board association.

105
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:42,720
Now, with that in mind, let's go back and read the text.

106
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:45,040
So we say that most cases are sporadic.

107
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:47,580
The sporadic form is seen in the elderly.

108
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:49,620
The key risk is age.

109
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:54,660
There's also an association with the epsilon-4 allele of apolipoprotein E.

110
00:07:55,040 --> 00:08:03,200
Of course, the epsilon-4 is associated with an increased risk, and the epsilon-2 allele is associated with a decreased risk.

111
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:06,620
Early onset Alzheimer's disease would be seen in younger patients.

112
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:14,580
One key association is that there is a set of familial cases, the major mutation here being in a gene called presenilin-1.

113
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:24,140
The second association here is Down syndrome, and the major issue here is that the amyloid precursor protein is present, of course, on chromosome 21,

114
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:26,980
and so that's particularly high yield to be aware of.

115
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:33,540
When a patient develops Alzheimer's disease, there are a few classic pathologic features that will arise within the brain.

116
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,000
Now, it doesn't matter whether you're talking about the sporadic form or the early form.

117
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:40,520
The pathologic features are similar.

118
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:46,400
However, we do need to be aware of them, and so this first image highlights one important pathologic feature,

119
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:51,220
and that is that you get diffuse cerebral atrophy of the brain.

120
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:55,340
Now, very important to note that this cerebral atrophy will be diffuse.

121
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:58,880
When you get this atrophy, a few important changes occur.

122
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:02,880
First of all, can you see these folds running across the brain here?

123
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:09,320
These are called the gyri, and one of the key findings is that you're going to get narrowing of these gyri,

124
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:11,960
and so that's one of the hallmarks of atrophy.

125
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:19,260
In addition, notice that you've got these spaces here between the gyri, here and here, for example, and here.

126
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:24,360
These are called the salsi, and so the second finding will be that you'll get widening of the salsi.

127
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:30,200
So, again, diffuse atrophy with narrowing of the gyri and widening of the salsi.

128
00:09:30,820 --> 00:09:35,980
Now, a consequence of this atrophy, which is not shown on this slide but is important to know,

129
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:42,300
is that because you're losing brain mass, the ventricles are actually going to dilate and take up some of that space.

130
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:49,960
And so when you get dilatation of the ventricles due to loss of brain mass, we call that hydrocephalus ex-vacuole.

131
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:53,520
And that's an important point to be aware of, so that in this particular case,

132
00:09:53,620 --> 00:09:56,020
were we to cut through this brain and look at the ventricles,

133
00:09:56,340 --> 00:10:01,740
we would notice that there is dilation of the ventricles, and so, again, hydrocephalus ex-vacuole.

134
00:10:01,740 --> 00:10:04,040
Now, that's not due to any increase in CSF.

135
00:10:04,380 --> 00:10:07,840
Instead, it's actually due to atrophy of the brain with loss of parenchyma.

136
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:14,060
The next important pathologic finding is that there will be development of something called a neuritic plaque.

137
00:10:14,420 --> 00:10:19,740
Now, here, I'm highlighting for you a neuritic plaque, and a neuritic plaque has two major components.

138
00:10:20,220 --> 00:10:30,020
The first is that it has A-beta amyloid, and so you can see that there is this pink material, which represents amyloid.

139
00:10:30,020 --> 00:10:34,740
Of course, this material is extracellular, because amyloid is always extracellular.

140
00:10:35,140 --> 00:10:40,020
And this pink material, as it deposits, it begins to entrap neuritic processes,

141
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:44,000
so that within here, you're also going to have neuritic processes.

142
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:51,060
And so we call this whole thing a neuritic plaque, and it's a combination of A-beta amyloid with entrapped neuritic processes.

143
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:57,620
Now, important to note that this A-beta amyloid is actually derived from amyloid precursor protein.

144
00:10:57,620 --> 00:11:01,900
A second important point to note, which is not highlighted on this image,

145
00:11:02,140 --> 00:11:06,720
is that this A-beta amyloid can also deposit around blood vessels within the brain.

146
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:12,120
And when it deposits around blood vessels within the brain, we call that cerebral amyloid angiopathy.

147
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:16,660
Now, the problem with this deposition is that it then weakens the wall of the blood vessel,

148
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:19,480
which increases the risk for hemorrhage into the brain.

149
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:25,620
And so that's a particularly high-yield association as well, i.e. cerebral amyloid angiopathy.

150
00:11:25,620 --> 00:11:32,460
The third pathologic hallmark of Alzheimer's disease is the formation of something called neurofibrillary tangles.

151
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:35,360
Now, notice that I've highlighted for you here a neuron.

152
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:37,700
Now, this is the nucleus of the neuron.

153
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:42,620
And within this neuron, you can see all these delicate fibers running through the cytoplasm,

154
00:11:43,540 --> 00:11:47,440
which is sort of creating this flame-shaped edge to the cell.

155
00:11:47,440 --> 00:11:55,400
And so what's happening here is that you've got deposition of something called a neurofibrillary tangle within the cytoplasm of the cell.

156
00:11:55,900 --> 00:12:01,420
Now, board examiners like you to know that present within this neurofibrillary tangle is a protein called tau.

157
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:05,240
Tau is a microtubule-associated protein.

158
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:10,720
It helps to organize the microtubules, which are part of the cytoskeleton, within the cytoplasm.

159
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:15,560
In this particular case, what happens is this tau becomes hyperphosphorylated.

160
00:12:16,080 --> 00:12:21,280
And when it becomes hyperphosphorylated, it can no longer assist in the organization of those microtubules.

161
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:26,100
And so you get these funny neurofibrillary tangles within the cytoplasm of the cell.

162
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:32,920
Now, board examiners like you to know that within the neurofibrillary tangle, it's going to be hyperphosphorylated tau.

163
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:34,820
And that's particularly high yield.

164
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:38,880
The diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease is a presumptive diagnosis.

165
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:43,820
It's made clinically, but only after excluding all other causes of dementia.

166
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:47,360
Now, the final confirmation, however, requires pathology.

167
00:12:47,860 --> 00:12:49,660
So it would be done at autopsy.

168
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:52,120
However, of course, autopsy is often not available.

169
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:58,040
But the true final diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease is actually both clinical and pathologic.

170
00:12:58,040 --> 00:13:02,740
And you put all of that data together to give a final true diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease.

171
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:05,900
An additional type of dementia is called vascular dementia.

172
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:09,380
The idea here is that you've got a problem with the blood vessels.

173
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:15,100
For example, hypertension, atherosclerosis, vasculitis, which decreases blood flow to the brain.

174
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:19,620
That decreased blood flow to the brain will result in multifocal infarction and injury.

175
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:21,740
And that will eventually result in dementia.

176
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:28,420
Now, the way I think about this is to simply go back and remind myself that when you have global cerebral ischemia,

177
00:13:28,420 --> 00:13:32,500
which, again, was one of the cerebrovascular disorders that we covered,

178
00:13:32,920 --> 00:13:37,840
I told you at that point that you can get mild, moderate, or severe global cerebral ischemia.

179
00:13:38,300 --> 00:13:44,120
And in the case of moderate, I told you that you would get damage to areas of selective vulnerability.

180
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:50,060
For example, one of the areas that was going to get damaged was layers 3, 5, and 6 of the cortex,

181
00:13:50,260 --> 00:13:51,960
which represents the pyramidal neurons.

182
00:13:52,400 --> 00:13:55,360
Now, imagine if you're knocking out pyramidal neurons from the cortex,

183
00:13:55,480 --> 00:13:57,920
what you're really doing is knocking out the gray matter of the cortex.

184
00:13:58,400 --> 00:14:00,540
And, of course, that could eventually result in a dementia.

185
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:07,480
In addition, recall that I told you that another select region of damage would be the hippocampus of the temporal lobe.

186
00:14:07,740 --> 00:14:11,700
Now, when you damage the hippocampus of the temporal lobe, you're going to create problems with memory.

187
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:15,120
And, of course, memory is also an essential component of dementia.

188
00:14:15,740 --> 00:14:22,820
And so vascular dementia, one way to think about it, is that it is a consequence of moderate global cerebral ischemia.

189
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:26,180
And, of course, this is important because if it's a vascular dementia,

190
00:14:26,340 --> 00:14:32,160
you might be able to alleviate the vascular problem and, therefore, improve the patient's symptoms and prognosis.

191
00:14:32,420 --> 00:14:37,480
So this is important to know that it is a relatively common cause of dementia in the elderly.

192
00:14:37,480 --> 00:14:41,820
Picks' disease is another degenerative disease of the cortex.

193
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:47,180
However, what's important to note is that this predominantly involves the frontal and temporal cortex

194
00:14:47,180 --> 00:14:50,140
and spares the parietal and occipital lobes.

195
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:55,540
The way I think about this is that in Picks' disease, it picks the frontal and temporal cortex.

196
00:14:56,100 --> 00:15:01,120
Now, this is important because, remember, in Alzheimer's, we had diffuse atrophy of the cortex.

197
00:15:01,460 --> 00:15:04,360
And here, it's going to be the frontal and temporal cortex.

198
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:08,280
Now, the consequence of this is that when you knock out the frontal cortex,

199
00:15:08,420 --> 00:15:10,080
you're going to develop problems with behavior.

200
00:15:10,520 --> 00:15:13,880
And when you knock out the temporal cortex, you're going to get problems with language.

201
00:15:14,760 --> 00:15:19,180
And so along with dementia, you get early behavioral and language symptoms.

202
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:21,800
Now, were you to examine the brain microscopically,

203
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:26,120
you would see round aggregates of tau protein within the neurons of the cortex.

204
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:30,420
Now, recall that tau protein is a microtubule-associated protein.

205
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:34,600
It helps the microtubules of the cytoskeleton arrange themselves properly.

206
00:15:35,180 --> 00:15:38,780
And in this particular case, you're going to get these round aggregates of tau protein.

207
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:44,240
Now, recall in Alzheimer's disease, we also had tau protein, which was forming neurofibrillary tangles.

208
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:46,200
But we didn't get round aggregates.

209
00:15:46,260 --> 00:15:47,920
We got the neurofibrillary tangles.

210
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:50,820
In this particular disorder, we have tau protein.

211
00:15:51,180 --> 00:15:53,680
Instead, it's going to produce these round aggregates.

212
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:55,900
And that's particularly high yield as well.

213
00:15:55,900 --> 00:16:02,140
So, switching gears a little bit, Parkinson's disease is a degenerative disorder in which you lose neurons.

214
00:16:02,520 --> 00:16:05,320
However, instead of losing neurons predominantly in the cortex,

215
00:16:05,820 --> 00:16:09,300
you actually lose neurons in the deeper gray matter structures of the brain.

216
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:14,460
Now, one of the most important gray matter structures in the deep brain is the basal ganglia.

217
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,540
The basal ganglia helps to regulate movement.

218
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,280
We'll talk about that in a minute.

219
00:16:19,280 --> 00:16:25,300
And in this particular case, i.e. Parkinson's disease, you are going to lose the dopaminergic neurons

220
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:30,720
in the substantia niagara pars compacta, which is part of the basal ganglia.

221
00:16:31,200 --> 00:16:33,860
Now, in order to really understand this, we have to go to the whiteboard.

222
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:39,100
Let's remind ourselves that the cortex communicates with the basal ganglia.

223
00:16:39,100 --> 00:16:44,140
And the basal ganglia then sends its input back into the cortex.

224
00:16:45,220 --> 00:16:48,820
And the purpose of this loop is to help to regulate movement.

225
00:16:49,340 --> 00:16:52,600
Now, the key processing center of the basal ganglia is called the striatum.

226
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:57,080
And what you need to know about the striatum is that the striatum has the ability to send

227
00:16:57,080 --> 00:17:03,980
stimulatory signal to the cortex or inhibitory signal to the cortex.

228
00:17:03,980 --> 00:17:09,400
Now, in addition to receiving input from the cortex, the striatum also receives input

229
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:13,340
from the substantia niagara pars compacta.

230
00:17:13,660 --> 00:17:17,840
And the dopaminergic neurons in particular of the substantia niagara pars compacta

231
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:18,940
will release dopamine.

232
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:25,000
Now, when they release dopamine, binding of D1 receptors on the striatum

233
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,780
will result in extra stimulation to the cortex.

234
00:17:28,780 --> 00:17:34,160
And binding of D2 receptors will result in decreased inhibition of the cortex.

235
00:17:34,580 --> 00:17:38,780
And so the overall effect of dopamine is to increase stimulation to the cortex

236
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:40,640
and decrease inhibition to the cortex.

237
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,060
And both of those are going to work to increase cortical function

238
00:17:44,060 --> 00:17:45,820
so that you can increase movement.

239
00:17:46,280 --> 00:17:50,640
And so the substantia niagara pars compacta has an effect on the striatum.

240
00:17:50,760 --> 00:17:54,140
This whole thing, by the way, is called the niagara striatal pathway.

241
00:17:54,140 --> 00:17:59,980
And this pathway helps to initiate movement by increasing the overall signal to the cortex.

242
00:18:00,440 --> 00:18:05,820
Now, imagine if you knock out the dopaminergic neurons of the substantia niagara pars compacta,

243
00:18:06,060 --> 00:18:07,940
which is what happens in Parkinson's disease,

244
00:18:08,520 --> 00:18:11,280
you're not going to get this increased stimulation to the cortex.

245
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:15,540
And that's going to result in the movement disorders that are seen in Parkinson's disease.

246
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:18,400
Now, with that background, we can go back and read the text.

247
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:24,120
And so, again, I tell you that Parkinson's disease is a degenerative loss of the dopaminergic neurons

248
00:18:24,120 --> 00:18:28,020
in the substantia niagara pars compacta, which is part of the basal ganglia.

249
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:30,480
This is a relatively common disorder.

250
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:31,980
The key risk factor is aging.

251
00:18:32,480 --> 00:18:34,060
We don't know the etiology.

252
00:18:34,900 --> 00:18:40,200
Historically, however, there were rare cases related to exposure to this molecule called MPTP,

253
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:42,600
which was a contaminant in illicit drugs.

254
00:18:42,600 --> 00:18:47,360
And board examiners like you to know that MPTP can result in Parkinson's disease.

255
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:51,740
The clinical features of Parkinson's disease are all based on the fact that you've knocked out

256
00:18:51,740 --> 00:18:57,540
the dopaminergic input to the striatum, in particular, the dopaminergic neurons of the substantia niagara.

257
00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:00,820
And, of course, with that decreased input to the striatum,

258
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:02,720
you're going to have decreased input to the cortex,

259
00:19:02,920 --> 00:19:05,100
and so you're not going to be able to generate movement as well.

260
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:07,460
In particular, these patients get tremor.

261
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:10,820
It's a pill-rolling tremor at rest that disappears with movement.

262
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:12,160
They get rigidity.

263
00:19:12,380 --> 00:19:15,060
This is this cogwheel rigidity of their extremities.

264
00:19:15,060 --> 00:19:20,000
They get akinesia, bradykinesia, which is slowing of all voluntary movements.

265
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:23,260
One example of this would be that they get an expressionless face,

266
00:19:23,320 --> 00:19:27,500
as if they can't move even the muscles of the face to smile, for example.

267
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:32,440
And then they can get a shuffling gait, again, because they're not able to establish their movement,

268
00:19:32,760 --> 00:19:34,480
and they get postural instability.

269
00:19:35,060 --> 00:19:39,140
All of this can be remembered by the mnemonic TRAP, T-R-A-P.

270
00:19:39,140 --> 00:19:43,000
Here's a picture of the pigmented neurons of the substantia niagara,

271
00:19:43,140 --> 00:19:48,340
and you can see that here you've got the normal pigmented neurons present within the substantia niagara.

272
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:51,380
Here's a specimen from a patient with Parkinson's disease.

273
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:55,560
Notice that you've lost the pigmented neurons in the substantia niagara,

274
00:19:55,740 --> 00:20:00,600
in particular, the dopaminergic neurons in the substantia niagara pars compacta.

275
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:04,140
The key finding is that within the neurons of the substantia niagara,

276
00:20:04,520 --> 00:20:08,720
you're going to see these round eosinophilic inclusions, which are called Lewy bodies.

277
00:20:09,260 --> 00:20:11,640
Now here, I've highlighted for you a neuron,

278
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:15,280
and notice that within this neuron, you've got this round structure,

279
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:17,200
which is called a Lewy body.

280
00:20:17,560 --> 00:20:22,820
And board examiners like you to know that this Lewy body is composed of alpha-synuclein,

281
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:23,800
very high yield.

282
00:20:24,180 --> 00:20:27,320
This round Lewy body is composed of alpha-synuclein,

283
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:29,840
and again, that's the hallmark of Parkinson's disease.

284
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:32,440
One last high yield point is that over time,

285
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:34,720
patients with Parkinson's can develop dementia.

286
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:36,060
It's relatively common.

287
00:20:36,340 --> 00:20:38,440
However, it is seen late in the disease.

288
00:20:38,760 --> 00:20:40,520
For example, years into the disease.

289
00:20:41,060 --> 00:20:43,700
If a patient has early onset dementia,

290
00:20:43,860 --> 00:20:46,120
for example, within the first year of diagnosis,

291
00:20:46,620 --> 00:20:50,040
that suggests a different disorder, which is called Lewy body dementia.

292
00:20:50,580 --> 00:20:52,720
And what's important about Lewy body dementia is that,

293
00:20:52,800 --> 00:20:55,680
of course, you get dementia with Parkinson's-like features.

294
00:20:55,680 --> 00:20:57,500
They can also have hallucinations.

295
00:20:57,500 --> 00:21:00,400
However, they get cortical Lewy bodies,

296
00:21:00,560 --> 00:21:05,020
which means that they have a lot of Lewy bodies within the gray matter of the cortex.

297
00:21:05,280 --> 00:21:06,900
And, of course, when you knock out the cortex,

298
00:21:07,060 --> 00:21:08,500
that's what's going to give you the dementia.

299
00:21:09,100 --> 00:21:11,720
So, late dementia is part of Parkinson's disease.

300
00:21:12,220 --> 00:21:13,580
Early dementia, for example,

301
00:21:13,700 --> 00:21:16,460
within one year of the development of Parkinsonian features,

302
00:21:16,800 --> 00:21:18,540
would not be called Parkinson's disease.

303
00:21:18,960 --> 00:21:20,940
Instead, it would be called Lewy body dementia,

304
00:21:20,940 --> 00:21:25,140
and that's because you've got Lewy bodies in the cortex of the brain.

305
00:21:26,200 --> 00:21:29,900
Huntington's disease is another degenerative disorder of the basal ganglia.

306
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:31,700
However, in this particular case,

307
00:21:31,780 --> 00:21:36,920
you knock out the GABAergic neurons in the caudate nucleus of the basal ganglia.

308
00:21:37,440 --> 00:21:38,880
Now, this requires a little bit of review,

309
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:40,760
so let's remind ourselves that the cortex

310
00:21:40,760 --> 00:21:45,220
is going to provide signal to the basal ganglia.

311
00:21:45,840 --> 00:21:51,220
And, in particular, remember we said that it was the striatum of the basal ganglia.

312
00:21:51,780 --> 00:21:54,960
And the basal ganglia, i.e. the striatum of the basal ganglia,

313
00:21:55,080 --> 00:21:57,020
is then going to send message back to the cortex,

314
00:21:57,360 --> 00:22:00,260
which is going to help to regulate movement.

315
00:22:00,820 --> 00:22:03,200
Now, what's important to note is the striatum

316
00:22:03,200 --> 00:22:06,500
is actually composed of two major gray matter structures.

317
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:10,360
The first is the caudate, and the second is the putamen.

318
00:22:10,760 --> 00:22:14,320
So, it's the caudate plus the putamen that forms the striatum.

319
00:22:14,860 --> 00:22:18,340
Now, imagine if you knock out the GABAergic neurons of the caudate,

320
00:22:18,420 --> 00:22:20,860
you're going to lose an important component of the striatum,

321
00:22:21,180 --> 00:22:23,940
and this is then going to result in a movement disorder

322
00:22:23,940 --> 00:22:25,800
because you've damaged the basal ganglia.

323
00:22:26,540 --> 00:22:28,260
Now, one additional high-yield point to know,

324
00:22:28,320 --> 00:22:29,940
and board examiners like to go after it,

325
00:22:30,120 --> 00:22:34,160
is that GABA is an inhibitory neurotransmitter.

326
00:22:34,160 --> 00:22:37,840
So, that the GABAergic neurons of the caudate nucleus

327
00:22:37,840 --> 00:22:41,200
actually provide an inhibitory signal to the cortex.

328
00:22:41,680 --> 00:22:44,420
Now, the idea here is that your cortex is designed to move

329
00:22:44,420 --> 00:22:45,660
and fire muscle very quickly.

330
00:22:46,100 --> 00:22:47,800
Basically, imagine if you're playing baseball

331
00:22:47,800 --> 00:22:50,460
and you need to quickly move to be able to catch the ball.

332
00:22:50,740 --> 00:22:52,040
You don't want to have to think about it.

333
00:22:52,160 --> 00:22:54,300
You want to be able to have the circuit fire

334
00:22:54,300 --> 00:22:55,780
and the muscles move very quickly.

335
00:22:56,380 --> 00:22:59,560
However, you also don't want random firing of the circuit

336
00:22:59,560 --> 00:23:02,120
so that muscle begins to fly in different directions.

337
00:23:02,120 --> 00:23:05,940
And so, you've got this inhibitory hold that's necessary

338
00:23:05,940 --> 00:23:09,160
which occurs via the GABAergic neurons.

339
00:23:09,560 --> 00:23:11,600
Now, imagine if you lose the GABAergic neurons,

340
00:23:11,720 --> 00:23:13,260
you lose the inhibitory hold,

341
00:23:13,540 --> 00:23:15,440
and when you lose the inhibitory hold,

342
00:23:15,540 --> 00:23:16,720
what's going to happen is you're going to get

343
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:18,460
this random firing of the system

344
00:23:18,460 --> 00:23:20,760
which is going to lead to random movements of muscle,

345
00:23:21,140 --> 00:23:22,320
which is called chorea.

346
00:23:22,660 --> 00:23:24,560
And so, the key problem in Huntington's disease

347
00:23:24,560 --> 00:23:29,260
is you lose the GABAergic neurons of the caudate nucleus.

348
00:23:29,260 --> 00:23:31,180
When you lose the GABAergic neurons,

349
00:23:31,300 --> 00:23:34,520
you lose the inhibitory hold on the cortex.

350
00:23:34,880 --> 00:23:36,960
When you lose the inhibitory hold on the cortex,

351
00:23:37,200 --> 00:23:39,420
you're going to get random firing of the system

352
00:23:39,420 --> 00:23:42,280
which is going to result in random purposeless movements

353
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:44,820
of the muscles, which is then called chorea.

354
00:23:45,160 --> 00:23:46,780
Now, let's go back and review the text.

355
00:23:47,320 --> 00:23:48,880
And so, I say that in Huntington's disease,

356
00:23:49,080 --> 00:23:51,060
you get loss of the GABAergic neurons

357
00:23:51,060 --> 00:23:52,140
in the caudate nucleus,

358
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:55,440
and of course, that's a key component of the basal ganglia.

359
00:23:55,440 --> 00:23:57,920
This is a normal coronal section of brain,

360
00:23:58,060 --> 00:23:59,200
and from this, I'd like to highlight

361
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:01,160
a few important principles of neural anatomy.

362
00:24:01,700 --> 00:24:03,420
Now, of course, this is the gray matter of the cortex.

363
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:05,000
This is the white matter of the cortex.

364
00:24:05,660 --> 00:24:08,480
These are the deeper gray matter structures of the brain.

365
00:24:09,100 --> 00:24:12,040
Notice that you've got two ventricular spaces here and here.

366
00:24:12,380 --> 00:24:14,400
So, therefore, this must be the lateral ventricle,

367
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:16,840
and this must also be the lateral ventricle.

368
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:19,100
And one thing that I'd like to highlight here

369
00:24:19,100 --> 00:24:21,640
is notice that you've got this gray matter structure here,

370
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:26,660
which is pushing in and indenting the surface of the lateral ventricle.

371
00:24:27,160 --> 00:24:28,800
Now, when you've got a big gray matter structure

372
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:31,740
that pushes in and indents the surface of the lateral ventricle,

373
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:33,960
that tells you that that's the caudate nucleus.

374
00:24:34,580 --> 00:24:35,560
Now, underneath the caudate,

375
00:24:35,640 --> 00:24:37,140
what you're seeing here is the putamen,

376
00:24:37,620 --> 00:24:40,280
and you've got this stripe of internal capsule

377
00:24:40,280 --> 00:24:42,340
going between the caudate and the putamen,

378
00:24:42,820 --> 00:24:45,780
and this entire region here is called the striatum.

379
00:24:45,900 --> 00:24:49,280
Basically, the caudate plus the putamen equals the striatum.

380
00:24:49,280 --> 00:24:52,080
It's called the striatum because you've got this stripe

381
00:24:52,080 --> 00:24:53,740
running through the center of it.

382
00:24:54,100 --> 00:24:56,700
Now, of course, the striatum is the major processing center

383
00:24:56,700 --> 00:24:59,700
of the basal ganga, which receives input from the cortex

384
00:24:59,700 --> 00:25:01,560
and then sends input back to the cortex,

385
00:25:01,680 --> 00:25:03,180
and we've already described that.

386
00:25:03,640 --> 00:25:06,640
Now, in this caudate nucleus, you've got GABAergic neurons,

387
00:25:07,180 --> 00:25:09,540
and these have an inhibitory hold on the cortex

388
00:25:09,540 --> 00:25:11,560
so that you don't randomly fire the system.

389
00:25:11,920 --> 00:25:13,740
When you lose the GABAergic neurons,

390
00:25:13,820 --> 00:25:15,500
you're going to lose that inhibitory hold,

391
00:25:15,820 --> 00:25:17,760
and so you're going to get random firing of the system,

392
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:20,300
which is going to result in random purposeless movements,

393
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:21,520
i.e. chorea.

394
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:24,140
Again, it's all due to a degeneration

395
00:25:24,140 --> 00:25:27,620
of the GABAergic neurons here in the caudate nucleus.

396
00:25:28,180 --> 00:25:30,860
Now, here's a coronal section from a patient with Huntington's.

397
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:33,980
Notice that you've lost the caudate nucleus here and here.

398
00:25:34,300 --> 00:25:36,460
Of course, here's the putamen and here's the putamen,

399
00:25:36,900 --> 00:25:38,420
and when you lose the caudate nucleus,

400
00:25:38,560 --> 00:25:41,400
the ventricle opens up because there's more space

401
00:25:41,400 --> 00:25:42,940
because you've lost mass in the brain,

402
00:25:42,940 --> 00:25:44,960
and so you get dilation of the ventricles,

403
00:25:45,100 --> 00:25:47,740
which, again, is called hydrocephalus ex-vacuole.

404
00:25:48,040 --> 00:25:50,200
It is not due to an increase in CSF.

405
00:25:50,280 --> 00:25:51,880
It's due to a loss of brain mass,

406
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:54,500
and so the key finding, again, in Huntington's disease

407
00:25:54,500 --> 00:25:57,200
is degeneration of the caudate nucleus,

408
00:25:57,460 --> 00:26:00,520
in particular, the GABAergic neurons of the caudate nucleus.

409
00:26:01,080 --> 00:26:03,740
Important to note that this is an autosomal dominant disorder.

410
00:26:04,160 --> 00:26:06,580
It's due to an expanded trinucleotide repeat,

411
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:08,080
particularly CAG.

412
00:26:08,220 --> 00:26:10,960
You need to know CAG in the Huntington gene.

413
00:26:10,960 --> 00:26:14,360
One very important feature of trinucleotide repeat diseases

414
00:26:14,360 --> 00:26:16,500
is that you can often see anticipation,

415
00:26:17,260 --> 00:26:19,920
which means that you can see the disease occur earlier

416
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:21,220
in subsequent generations.

417
00:26:21,400 --> 00:26:23,980
So, for example, the grandfather gets it at the age of 60,

418
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:26,400
but the father gets it at the age of 55,

419
00:26:26,820 --> 00:26:29,660
and so the child anticipates getting the disease earlier,

420
00:26:29,800 --> 00:26:31,280
for example, at the age of 50.

421
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:33,220
Now, board examiners like you to know

422
00:26:33,220 --> 00:26:37,240
that anticipation is due to further expansion

423
00:26:37,240 --> 00:26:40,680
of the trinucleotide repeat and very high yield.

424
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:44,040
That expansion occurs during spermatogenesis.

425
00:26:44,380 --> 00:26:45,680
That's particularly high yield.

426
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:48,020
Of course, patients with Huntington's disease

427
00:26:48,020 --> 00:26:49,040
are gonna have chorea.

428
00:26:49,440 --> 00:26:52,980
Chorea is a rapid involuntary contraction of muscle.

429
00:26:53,440 --> 00:26:55,640
They can also get something called athetosis.

430
00:26:55,880 --> 00:26:57,940
Athetosis is when you get these slow,

431
00:26:58,180 --> 00:27:00,840
involuntary, snake-like movements of the fingers.

432
00:27:01,340 --> 00:27:04,420
Board examiners like you to know about athetosis as well.

433
00:27:04,420 --> 00:27:06,760
This can eventually progress to dementia,

434
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:08,220
and so you could have dementia

435
00:27:08,220 --> 00:27:10,920
as a component of Huntington's disease.

436
00:27:11,300 --> 00:27:13,260
Eventually, patients also can get depression,

437
00:27:13,700 --> 00:27:15,840
and suicide is a common cause of death.

438
00:27:16,420 --> 00:27:18,480
Moving on to normal pressure hydrocephalus,

439
00:27:18,700 --> 00:27:21,040
this is a possible cause of dementia in adults.

440
00:27:21,460 --> 00:27:24,500
Now, the idea here is that you get increased CSF,

441
00:27:24,800 --> 00:27:26,720
which then dilates the ventricles,

442
00:27:26,980 --> 00:27:28,900
and when you get dilation of the ventricles,

443
00:27:29,160 --> 00:27:31,900
you get stretching of the corona radiata,

444
00:27:31,900 --> 00:27:33,980
which are the nerve fibers that are running

445
00:27:33,980 --> 00:27:35,600
along the edge of the ventricles.

446
00:27:36,020 --> 00:27:38,120
And so when you get stretching of all these nerve fibers,

447
00:27:38,440 --> 00:27:40,800
that's gonna result in a classic triad.

448
00:27:41,180 --> 00:27:43,320
And that triad is urinary incontinence,

449
00:27:43,720 --> 00:27:45,440
gait instability, and dementia.

450
00:27:46,040 --> 00:27:48,080
Now, one of the mnemonics that students like to use

451
00:27:48,080 --> 00:27:49,700
is wet, wacky, and wobbly.

452
00:27:49,960 --> 00:27:52,340
And of course, the wet refers to urinary incontinence,

453
00:27:52,640 --> 00:27:54,700
the wobbly refers to the gait instability,

454
00:27:55,100 --> 00:27:56,840
and the wacky to the dementia.

455
00:27:57,520 --> 00:27:59,560
Now, because this is due to increased CSF,

456
00:27:59,560 --> 00:28:01,720
one of the hallmarks of this disorder is that when you do

457
00:28:01,720 --> 00:28:04,180
a lumbar puncture, the symptoms will improve.

458
00:28:04,300 --> 00:28:05,900
That's particularly high yield.

459
00:28:06,200 --> 00:28:07,580
And taking this one step further,

460
00:28:07,720 --> 00:28:10,220
the treatment then would be to shunt CSF

461
00:28:10,220 --> 00:28:12,540
from the ventricles into the peritoneum,

462
00:28:12,840 --> 00:28:14,200
and that's called a VP shunt.

463
00:28:14,720 --> 00:28:16,380
Now, the last point that I'd like to make

464
00:28:16,380 --> 00:28:19,060
is why is there an increased CSF in these patients?

465
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:20,960
Well, let's go back and remind ourselves

466
00:28:20,960 --> 00:28:23,660
that the CSF is produced by the choroid plexus

467
00:28:23,660 --> 00:28:24,980
that lines the ventricles,

468
00:28:24,980 --> 00:28:27,660
and that the CSF is drained

469
00:28:27,660 --> 00:28:30,340
by something called arachnoid granulations.

470
00:28:31,080 --> 00:28:32,140
Let's go back.

471
00:28:32,720 --> 00:28:33,980
Here is the pia.

472
00:28:34,200 --> 00:28:35,340
This is the arachnoid.

473
00:28:35,420 --> 00:28:36,160
This is the dura.

474
00:28:36,600 --> 00:28:37,980
There are these arachnoid granulations,

475
00:28:38,680 --> 00:28:40,480
which are outpouchings of the arachnoid

476
00:28:40,480 --> 00:28:41,580
that go through the dura

477
00:28:41,580 --> 00:28:44,180
to access the venous sinuses here.

478
00:28:44,780 --> 00:28:47,340
Now, what happens is the CSF goes up

479
00:28:47,340 --> 00:28:48,980
into the arachnoid granulations

480
00:28:49,560 --> 00:28:52,020
and then gets resorbed into the venous sinuses.

481
00:28:52,020 --> 00:28:54,100
In normal pressure hydrocephalus,

482
00:28:54,360 --> 00:28:55,460
for some unknown reason,

483
00:28:55,640 --> 00:28:57,880
there is decreased absorption of CSF

484
00:28:57,880 --> 00:29:00,140
into the arachnoid granulations.

485
00:29:00,740 --> 00:29:03,160
And so that then results in increased CSF,

486
00:29:03,420 --> 00:29:05,680
which then results in dilation of the ventricles,

487
00:29:05,980 --> 00:29:07,280
which then results in stretching

488
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:10,520
of the corona radiata, high yield,

489
00:29:10,760 --> 00:29:12,460
stretching of the corona radiata,

490
00:29:12,800 --> 00:29:14,720
which then results in the classic triad

491
00:29:14,720 --> 00:29:16,960
of wet, wacky, and wobbly.

492
00:29:17,480 --> 00:29:18,940
The last disorder that we're going to cover

493
00:29:18,940 --> 00:29:20,520
is spongiform encephalopathy.

494
00:29:20,520 --> 00:29:22,640
This is also a degenerative disease.

495
00:29:22,800 --> 00:29:25,400
However, it's due to accumulation of prion protein.

496
00:29:25,980 --> 00:29:27,100
Now, in order to understand this,

497
00:29:27,160 --> 00:29:28,260
we've got to take a step back

498
00:29:28,260 --> 00:29:30,640
and recall that all of us in our brain

499
00:29:30,640 --> 00:29:32,020
have prion protein,

500
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:36,060
and it's normally in the alpha helical configuration.

501
00:29:36,560 --> 00:29:38,020
Now, we call that PRPC,

502
00:29:38,640 --> 00:29:40,560
and I've written that here on the board.

503
00:29:41,020 --> 00:29:45,400
Now, if this PRP gets converted to PRPSC,

504
00:29:45,400 --> 00:29:50,200
which basically is in a beta-pleated sheet configuration,

505
00:29:50,780 --> 00:29:54,520
that then results in production of a pathologic protein,

506
00:29:54,820 --> 00:29:57,120
which then causes spongiform encephalopathy.

507
00:29:57,620 --> 00:29:59,280
Now, this pathologic protein

508
00:29:59,280 --> 00:30:02,720
in the beta-pleated conformation creates two problems.

509
00:30:03,100 --> 00:30:05,420
Number one, it will go back to the alpha

510
00:30:05,420 --> 00:30:09,620
and also generate additional beta-pleated conformation,

511
00:30:09,620 --> 00:30:13,240
so it helps to generate more of the pathologic protein.

512
00:30:13,740 --> 00:30:16,020
And number two, when you get this pathologic protein,

513
00:30:16,180 --> 00:30:17,640
it'll accumulate in the neurons.

514
00:30:17,740 --> 00:30:18,720
You can't degrade it.

515
00:30:19,020 --> 00:30:20,220
And when you can't degrade it,

516
00:30:20,400 --> 00:30:22,560
that then is going to result in damage to the neurons,

517
00:30:22,860 --> 00:30:24,460
and the classic damage to the neurons

518
00:30:24,460 --> 00:30:27,080
results in these vaculations in the cytoplasm,

519
00:30:27,340 --> 00:30:29,260
which produce these spongy holes

520
00:30:29,260 --> 00:30:32,060
or these white holes within the brain matter.

521
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:34,220
And I'll show you a picture of that in a minute.

522
00:30:34,560 --> 00:30:35,800
So this is the basic premise,

523
00:30:35,800 --> 00:30:36,600
and with this in mind,

524
00:30:36,660 --> 00:30:38,900
we can go back and discuss some of the features

525
00:30:38,900 --> 00:30:40,500
of spongiform encephalopathy.

526
00:30:40,960 --> 00:30:42,140
Now, I say here in the text

527
00:30:42,140 --> 00:30:43,920
that a spongiform encephalopathy

528
00:30:43,920 --> 00:30:45,360
is a degenerative disorder

529
00:30:45,360 --> 00:30:47,940
due to accumulation of prion protein.

530
00:30:48,500 --> 00:30:49,820
The pathologic protein,

531
00:30:50,020 --> 00:30:52,640
which again is in a beta-pleated conformation,

532
00:30:52,840 --> 00:30:53,580
that's high yield,

533
00:30:54,080 --> 00:30:55,560
it converts normal protein

534
00:30:55,560 --> 00:30:57,460
into additional pathologic form.

535
00:30:57,920 --> 00:30:59,840
And again, the problem with pathologic form

536
00:30:59,840 --> 00:31:01,160
is that it's not degradable.

537
00:31:01,640 --> 00:31:03,060
Now, because it's not degradable,

538
00:31:03,060 --> 00:31:05,800
it'll damage neurons and glial cells,

539
00:31:06,160 --> 00:31:07,580
and the characteristic hallmark

540
00:31:07,580 --> 00:31:09,560
will be intracellular vacuoles

541
00:31:09,560 --> 00:31:10,960
or spaces within the cells.

542
00:31:11,460 --> 00:31:13,860
Now, here's a picture of spongiform encephalopathy,

543
00:31:14,300 --> 00:31:17,220
and you can see all these white spongy spaces

544
00:31:17,220 --> 00:31:20,080
present within the brain parenchyma.

545
00:31:20,420 --> 00:31:21,500
And again, that's the highlight.

546
00:31:21,640 --> 00:31:23,000
You get spongy degeneration

547
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,160
of the parenchyma of the brain.

548
00:31:25,660 --> 00:31:26,840
The next discussion deals with

549
00:31:26,840 --> 00:31:28,060
how you can actually develop

550
00:31:28,060 --> 00:31:29,480
this beta-pleated conformation,

551
00:31:30,120 --> 00:31:31,500
meaning how is it that you go

552
00:31:31,500 --> 00:31:33,100
from the normal alpha conformation

553
00:31:33,100 --> 00:31:35,240
to the beta-pleated sheet conformation?

554
00:31:35,780 --> 00:31:36,560
And so the answer is

555
00:31:36,560 --> 00:31:37,540
that there are three ways.

556
00:31:37,880 --> 00:31:38,980
The first is sporadic,

557
00:31:39,060 --> 00:31:40,720
where you've got the alpha conformation,

558
00:31:40,940 --> 00:31:42,460
and it just sporadically converts

559
00:31:42,460 --> 00:31:43,400
to the beta form.

560
00:31:43,880 --> 00:31:45,200
Now, this sporadic conversion

561
00:31:45,200 --> 00:31:46,160
is a problem

562
00:31:46,160 --> 00:31:47,780
because that single beta form

563
00:31:47,780 --> 00:31:48,560
will then go back

564
00:31:48,560 --> 00:31:50,300
and convert other alpha proteins

565
00:31:50,300 --> 00:31:51,300
to beta as well.

566
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:52,820
The betas will pile up,

567
00:31:53,120 --> 00:31:54,160
and then the beta form

568
00:31:54,160 --> 00:31:55,440
will then eventually result

569
00:31:55,440 --> 00:31:57,040
in spongiform encephalopathy.

570
00:31:57,620 --> 00:31:58,640
A second way by which

571
00:31:58,640 --> 00:31:59,980
this transformation can occur

572
00:31:59,980 --> 00:32:02,060
is if you inherit a bad gene.

573
00:32:02,460 --> 00:32:03,300
Now, the idea here

574
00:32:03,300 --> 00:32:03,900
is that you've got

575
00:32:03,900 --> 00:32:05,480
a normal prion gene

576
00:32:05,480 --> 00:32:07,040
that produces a prion protein

577
00:32:07,040 --> 00:32:07,940
in your brain.

578
00:32:08,240 --> 00:32:09,820
If you inherit a bad gene,

579
00:32:10,200 --> 00:32:11,220
that gene can result

580
00:32:11,220 --> 00:32:12,800
in increased conversion

581
00:32:12,800 --> 00:32:13,860
of the alpha form

582
00:32:13,860 --> 00:32:15,340
to the beta-pleated conformation.

583
00:32:15,860 --> 00:32:16,180
Of course,

584
00:32:16,260 --> 00:32:16,580
when you get

585
00:32:16,580 --> 00:32:17,920
the beta-pleated conformation,

586
00:32:18,260 --> 00:32:19,080
that'll then convert

587
00:32:19,080 --> 00:32:20,280
other alphas to beta,

588
00:32:20,480 --> 00:32:20,880
giving you

589
00:32:20,880 --> 00:32:22,300
a spongiform encephalopathy.

590
00:32:22,940 --> 00:32:23,640
And the third way

591
00:32:23,640 --> 00:32:24,320
is transmitted.

592
00:32:24,760 --> 00:32:26,200
Imagine if you get exposed

593
00:32:26,200 --> 00:32:28,060
to the beta-pleated protein,

594
00:32:28,060 --> 00:32:29,660
that beta-pleated protein

595
00:32:29,660 --> 00:32:31,460
can then go into your brain,

596
00:32:31,860 --> 00:32:33,280
convert your alphas into beta,

597
00:32:33,580 --> 00:32:34,200
and then eventually

598
00:32:34,200 --> 00:32:35,460
cause the disease

599
00:32:35,460 --> 00:32:36,840
within the normal brain.

600
00:32:37,120 --> 00:32:38,160
And so that would be transmitted,

601
00:32:38,320 --> 00:32:38,720
and that's why

602
00:32:38,720 --> 00:32:39,780
we classically say

603
00:32:39,780 --> 00:32:40,700
that prion proteins

604
00:32:40,700 --> 00:32:42,040
can also be infectious,

605
00:32:42,260 --> 00:32:42,760
quote-unquote.

606
00:32:43,440 --> 00:32:44,360
The most common form

607
00:32:44,360 --> 00:32:45,740
of spongiform encephalopathy

608
00:32:45,740 --> 00:32:46,640
is CJD.

609
00:32:47,080 --> 00:32:48,620
This is usually sporadic,

610
00:32:48,720 --> 00:32:49,660
which means that it occurs

611
00:32:49,660 --> 00:32:51,260
via sporadic conversion

612
00:32:51,260 --> 00:32:52,420
of the alpha form

613
00:32:52,420 --> 00:32:53,660
to the beta-pleated sheet.

614
00:32:54,020 --> 00:32:55,720
However, it can rarely arise

615
00:32:55,720 --> 00:32:56,560
due to exposure

616
00:32:56,560 --> 00:32:59,180
to prion-infected human tissue.

617
00:32:59,620 --> 00:33:00,140
Now, the classic

618
00:33:00,140 --> 00:33:01,100
historical examples

619
00:33:01,100 --> 00:33:02,380
would be human growth hormone,

620
00:33:02,580 --> 00:33:03,940
which could have been derived

621
00:33:03,940 --> 00:33:04,600
from a patient

622
00:33:04,600 --> 00:33:05,740
who has, for example,

623
00:33:06,180 --> 00:33:07,060
the beta-protein,

624
00:33:07,280 --> 00:33:08,320
and that beta-protein

625
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:08,980
then gets put

626
00:33:08,980 --> 00:33:09,940
into another patient,

627
00:33:10,260 --> 00:33:11,560
or a corneal transplant,

628
00:33:12,020 --> 00:33:12,640
where the cornea

629
00:33:12,640 --> 00:33:13,840
is given from one patient

630
00:33:13,840 --> 00:33:15,060
who has the beta-protein

631
00:33:15,060 --> 00:33:16,320
to another normal patient.

632
00:33:16,840 --> 00:33:17,820
And this could potentially,

633
00:33:18,020 --> 00:33:18,600
although rarely,

634
00:33:18,720 --> 00:33:19,760
result in CJD.

635
00:33:20,180 --> 00:33:21,440
Now, the hallmark of CJD

636
00:33:21,440 --> 00:33:22,660
is rapid dementia,

637
00:33:22,840 --> 00:33:23,500
very high yield.

638
00:33:23,600 --> 00:33:24,420
The dementia develops

639
00:33:24,420 --> 00:33:25,660
within a very short period

640
00:33:25,660 --> 00:33:27,360
of time, weeks to months,

641
00:33:27,700 --> 00:33:28,500
with the patient

642
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:29,420
eventually dying,

643
00:33:29,540 --> 00:33:30,760
usually in less than a year.

644
00:33:31,260 --> 00:33:32,100
In addition to the

645
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:33,240
rapid progressive dementia,

646
00:33:33,360 --> 00:33:34,140
the patients can also

647
00:33:34,140 --> 00:33:34,920
get ataxia

648
00:33:34,920 --> 00:33:35,980
because the cerebellum

649
00:33:35,980 --> 00:33:36,660
can be involved,

650
00:33:37,100 --> 00:33:37,980
and there can also be

651
00:33:37,980 --> 00:33:39,020
startle myoclonus.

652
00:33:39,400 --> 00:33:40,340
Startle myoclonus

653
00:33:40,340 --> 00:33:41,020
is when patients

654
00:33:41,020 --> 00:33:42,120
get involuntary

655
00:33:42,120 --> 00:33:43,280
contractions of muscle

656
00:33:43,280 --> 00:33:44,900
with minimal stimuli,

657
00:33:45,360 --> 00:33:45,940
and so that's called

658
00:33:45,940 --> 00:33:47,320
startle myoclonus.

659
00:33:47,820 --> 00:33:48,520
On EEG,

660
00:33:48,740 --> 00:33:49,620
a hallmark would be

661
00:33:49,620 --> 00:33:51,020
spike wave complexes,

662
00:33:51,220 --> 00:33:52,100
and this is particularly

663
00:33:52,100 --> 00:33:53,120
high yield as well.

664
00:33:53,120 --> 00:33:53,920
And of course,

665
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:54,620
as I've mentioned,

666
00:33:54,800 --> 00:33:55,800
the dementia is rapidly

667
00:33:55,800 --> 00:33:56,360
progressive,

668
00:33:56,840 --> 00:33:57,740
resulting in death,

669
00:33:58,060 --> 00:33:58,940
usually in less than

670
00:33:58,940 --> 00:33:59,440
one year.

671
00:33:59,960 --> 00:34:00,740
Now, there are a couple

672
00:34:00,740 --> 00:34:01,800
of other spongiform

673
00:34:01,800 --> 00:34:02,740
encephalopathies

674
00:34:02,740 --> 00:34:03,540
to be aware of.

675
00:34:03,840 --> 00:34:05,500
The first is variant CJD.

676
00:34:05,780 --> 00:34:07,400
This is exactly like CJD,

677
00:34:07,540 --> 00:34:07,800
however,

678
00:34:07,920 --> 00:34:08,920
there are two variations.

679
00:34:09,460 --> 00:34:10,040
Number one,

680
00:34:10,120 --> 00:34:11,480
it occurs in younger patients,

681
00:34:11,860 --> 00:34:12,660
and number two,

682
00:34:12,960 --> 00:34:14,440
it's linked to consumption

683
00:34:14,440 --> 00:34:15,980
of meat from cows

684
00:34:15,980 --> 00:34:16,860
that have spongiform

685
00:34:16,860 --> 00:34:17,720
encephalopathy,

686
00:34:18,180 --> 00:34:19,200
mad cow disease.

687
00:34:19,620 --> 00:34:20,180
And so therefore,

688
00:34:20,180 --> 00:34:21,180
these are the two key

689
00:34:21,180 --> 00:34:23,220
features of variant CJD.

690
00:34:23,580 --> 00:34:24,540
And another spongiform

691
00:34:24,540 --> 00:34:25,840
encephalopathy to be aware

692
00:34:25,840 --> 00:34:26,560
of is called

693
00:34:26,560 --> 00:34:28,280
familial fatal insomnia.

694
00:34:28,580 --> 00:34:30,040
This is an inherited form

695
00:34:30,040 --> 00:34:31,260
of prion disease,

696
00:34:31,520 --> 00:34:32,260
which is characterized

697
00:34:32,260 --> 00:34:33,280
by insomnia

698
00:34:33,280 --> 00:34:34,900
and an exaggerated

699
00:34:34,900 --> 00:34:36,040
startle response.

700
00:34:36,320 --> 00:34:37,060
And so these are two

701
00:34:37,060 --> 00:34:38,400
other types of spongiform

702
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:39,260
encephalopathy

703
00:34:39,260 --> 00:34:40,380
that are important

704
00:34:40,380 --> 00:34:41,220
to be aware of.

705
00:34:41,640 --> 00:34:42,300
And that concludes

706
00:34:42,300 --> 00:34:42,880
our discussion

707
00:34:42,880 --> 00:34:44,340
on degenerative disorders.

708
00:34:44,340 --> 00:34:45,740
Transcription by CastingWords

